            <ArticleTitle>Bronchiolitis associated with exposure to artificial butter flavoring in workers at a cookie factory in Brazil.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>395-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1806-37132012000300016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report the cases of four patients with bronchiolitis caused by exposure to artificial butter flavoring at a cookie factory in Brazil.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We described the clinical, tomographic, and spirometric findings in the four patients, as well as the lung biopsy findings in one of the patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All four patients were young male nonsmokers and developed persistent airflow obstruction (reduced FEV1/FVC ratio and FEV1 at 25-44% of predicted) after 1-3 years of exposure to diacetyl, without the use of personal protective equipment, at a cookie factory. The HRCT findings were indicative of bronchiolitis. In one patient, the surgical lung biopsy revealed bronchiolitis obliterans accompanied by giant cells.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Bronchiolitis resulting from exposure to artificial flavoring agents should be included in the differential diagnosis of airflow obstruction in workers in Brazil.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
